  "title": "Control of ciguatera poisoning in Canary Islands, Spain",
  "summary": "The surveillance system set up by the Canary Islands Government aims to remove certain fish containing ciguatoxins from the market and to improve the detection of ciguatera in humans. The case study illustrates benefits to artisanal fisheries and public health. Ciguatera poisoning (CP) occurs when people consume fish containing ciguatoxins (CTXs) with a high toxicity level. CTXs are a type of marine biotoxins produced by certain microalgae (Gambierdiscus spp . and Fukuyoa spp .) accumulated by the marine food chain.",
